Nethack is turn-based. Having a typo cause you damage seems like a
poor idea to me, especially since no one in her right mind would
intentionally select that option. As I see it: all the magical
fiddling is abstracted away, and the interface simply asks you in
advance which way you want to point it. If you type the wrong key, the
interface should ask you again.

Crawl is built very strongly around the idea that gameplay mistakes
are punished severely, while typos are not. That's part of why it
asks for confirmation before you step into deep water or lava (though
this is sometimes a good idea in Nethack, unlike Crawl) and lets you
cancel out almost any action before it takes a turn (e.g., if you
decide to cancel casting a spell while selecting a target, you don't
lose Pw; of course the spell failure chance is rolled only once the
decision to cast is irreversible.)

But hey, it's your variant. :)

